00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.580 align:start position:0%
European Union Finance and economy  ministers will discuss new rules aimed  at pre-empting bank crises and dealing  with problems like insolvency when they  happen at the next council in Brussels  the rules would require banks and  investment firms to draw up recovery  plans laying out the measures they would  take to restore their financial health  if things turn sour relevant authorities  would also have to prepare plans for  making a bank in trouble solvable again  the measures part of broader EU plans to  set up a banking union would establish  the order in which investors would help  bail out an institution and strife the  aim is to limit the use of taxpayer  money ministers will also work on  strengthening EU rules for taxes on  savings income the plans to make it  possible to tax all kinds of savings  income and products that generate  interest as well as to make it harder to  get around the regulations in place  currently member states are required to  exchange information so that interest  payments on savings made in one country  to people who live in another can be  taxed under the laws in the state of  residency the rules currently apply to  individual savers not companies the  ministers are likely to adopt a mandate  for negotiating strengthened savings tax  agreements with third countries like  Switzerland Liechtenstein and Monaco  they'll also pledge to step up the fight  against tax evasion and tax fraud and to  promote good practices and tax  governance in countries outside the EU
 

